Package: @alchemy.run/domain
Applies-to: vendor/alchemy/alchemy.run-domain-e1c3e6d.tgz
Upstream: not yet reported
Why: The prover reads `X.make(...)` of an event or a rejection, `constTrue` and `constFalse`, and a tagged union's
  `guards`, `isAnyOf`, `match` and `matchOrElse` itself, but not `Schema.is`: a `decide` or an `evolve` that asks
  `Schema.is(X)(value)` ends its proof with "calls a schema, which the prover does not model". And it answers a guard
  from what a value must fit, which is yes wherever it does not read the case's schema (a pattern, a check on a union),
  and reads a union told by a number as matching no case, where the library finds the case under the number's text.
  So the model would tell a case twice, once in its schema and once by hand, or prove a rule the real code breaks.
  With this the prover reads `Schema.is` of a domain class (by its name, as the library tells a class) and of a plain
  schema, a guard is `Schema.is` of its case, and where it cannot say exactly what a schema answers (a check it does
  not read or does not apply, a tag that is not text, a value it cannot see into) it says so and proves nothing.
Remove-when: The library's prover reads `Schema.is` and answers a guard exactly or not at all.
  `bun run beep patches prove` then says this patch's test passes without it.
Test: test/alchemy-domain/03-a-schemas-own-guards/SchemaGuards.test.ts

